#config.yaml 

Config.yaml is purpose to create new kind cluster.The kind tool lets you define a custom cluster via a YAML config file — especially useful for:

Creating multiple control-plane or worker nodes

Port-forwarding for services (like Jenkins on port 8080)

Mounting volumes

Enabling specific Kubernetes features

 kind: Cluster        
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4     -- image
nodes:
  - role: control-plane                -- master node
  - role: worker                       --  2 worker nodes
  - role: worker
    extraPortMappings:
      - containerPort: 30005           -- nodeport
        hostPort: 30005
------------------------------------------------------------------------
PVC (PersistentVolumeClaim)
Purpose:
To provide persistent storage to a pod (e.g., for Jenkins job configs, plugins, and build history).

Why use it:

Pods are ephemeral — if they restart, local data is lost.

PVC connects the pod to storage (EBS, disk, NFS, etc.) that survives restarts.

Example Use:
Jenkins stores all configurations in /var/jenkins_home. Without PVC, data would be lost on pod restart.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Service (svc)
Purpose:
To expose a pod to other parts of the cluster or to the outside world (depending on the type: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer).

Why use it:

Pods have dynamic IPs — services provide a stable endpoint.

Allows load balancing between multiple pod replicas.

Service Types:

ClusterIP: Internal only

NodePort: Exposes on host machine IP + port (e.g., localhost:30005)

LoadBalancer: For cloud environments (adds external IP)

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Deployment
Purpose:
To manage the lifecycle of pods, allowing replication, rolling updates, and self-healing (restart pods on failure).

Why use it:

You define how many replicas to run.

It will automatically replace crashed pods.

Supports version upgrades (rolling updates).

Easier to manage compared to raw pods.

Example Use:
Instead of manually managing Jenkins pods, use a Deployment to ensure 1 pod is always running, with auto-restart and upgrade control.

-------------------------------------------------------------------

#Commands used:

Creating all service : kubectl apply -f jenkins.yaml 

Creating name space : create ns <name_space>

Creating cluster : kind create cluster --config config.yaml --name surya 

To Get deltails of nodes : kubectl get nodes 

To Get details about all services : kubectl get all -n name_space

To get pods : kubectl get pods -o wide -n name_space

To login pod : kubectl exec --it pod/pod_name -- bash

To Post forwarding pod connection: kubectl port-forward service/jenkins-service 8080:8080


try connect your application : http://localhost:30005/

---------------------------------------------------------------

Why we need to Environment variables 

What Are Environment Variables?
Environment variables are key-value pairs that are injected into containers to configure application behavior without changing the code.

Why Use Environment Variables in Pods?
Configuration Management

Allows separating configuration from the application code.

> Easy to modify values like DB credentials, API keys, and feature flags without rebuilding the container image.

> Secrets and Sensitive Data

> Used to pass sensitive information such as:

> Database usernames/passwords

> Tokens or API keys

> Connection strings ,Portability & Scalability

> Same container image can be used in different environments (dev, staging, prod) by changing only the environment variables.

> Decouple from Infrastructure

> Apps don't need to know details about the infrastructure; they just read from environment variables.

> Easier CI/CD Integration

> Environments in CI/CD pipelines can pass environment-specific variables to the Pods dynamically.

Variables can control things like:

> Logging level (DEBUG, INFO)

Feature toggles

Region or environment name (ENV=prod)
#Defined in the Pod/Deployment YAML under env:
env:
  - name: DB_HOST
    value: "mysql-service"
  - name: DB_USER
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: db-credentials
        key: username
